(1) DOUBLING OF THE EXTREMALS AND NEAR-EXTREMALS
    minimum possible for a 13-set: |V+V| = 2*13-1 = 25, ratio 25/13 = 1.923
    family                 |V+V|  ratio    M          at floor?
    {1,...,13}              25  1.923   1/14       YES
    {1..11,13,24}           36  2.769   1/14       YES
    {1,...,12,14}           26  2.000   1/13       
    {1..11,13,25}           36  2.769   1/12       
    {2,...,14}              25  1.923   1/8        
    AP d=3                  25  1.923   13/38      
    odd {1,3,..,25}         25  1.923   1/2        

(2) DOES LARGE DOUBLING FORCE M AWAY FROM THE FLOOR?
    binned over random primitive 13-families
    doubling  n    min M      median M    all >= 1/13?
        4.0    4  0.148148   0.200000    yes
        4.5   14  0.129032   0.200000    yes
        5.0   62  0.135135   0.180328    yes
        5.5   10  0.150943   0.176471    yes

(3) THE SHARP TEST: is there a doubling threshold K with
    doubling(V) > K  =>  M(V) >= 1/13 (i.e. off the floor and past the gap)?
    families with M < 1/13: 0 of 60
      none -- every sampled family already clears 1/13

    extremal doublings for comparison: 1.923 (AP), 2.769 (second tight)
(4) THE MECHANISM: translation preserves doubling but destroys the floor
    family        doubling   M         q0   (q0 is NOT translation-invariant)
    {1,...,13}   1.923     1/14       14
    {2,...,14}   1.923     1/8        15
    {3,...,15}   1.923     1/6        16
    {4,...,16}   1.923     1/5        17
    {5,...,17}   1.923     5/22       18

    => all translates share doubling 1.923 (minimal), but M and q0 vary wildly.
       Any TRANSLATION-INVARIANT invariant is therefore blind to the floor,
       and doubling -- the Freiman invariant -- is translation-invariant.

(5) SAME POINT UNDER DILATION (which LRC DOES respect, THM-1050)
    1*{1..13}: doubling 1.923  M = 1/14  q0 = 14
    2*{1..13}: doubling 1.923  M = 1/14  q0 = 15
    3*{1..13}: doubling 1.923  M = 1/14  q0 = 14
    => dilation preserves BOTH doubling and M, consistent with THM-1050.

(6) THE DIVISIBILITY INVARIANT SEPARATES WHAT DOUBLING CANNOT
    {1,...,13}       doubling 1.923  q0 =  14  M = 1/14
    {2,...,14}       doubling 1.923  q0 =  15  M = 1/8
    {1..11,13,24}    doubling 2.769  q0 =  14  M = 1/14
    AP d=3           doubling 1.923  q0 =   3  M = 13/38
